\title{The Biquadratic Type Channel: Two Types, Full Pinning,\\ and a Swap-Symmetry Law}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the splitting behaviour of the quartic $f(x)=x^4-10x^2+1$, the minimal polynomial of $\sqrt2+\sqrt3$, modulo primes, and we quantify it information-theoretically. The splitting field is the biquadratic field $\Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt3)$, with Galois group $V_4=C_2\times C_2$ and conductor $24$. We first prove a field-independent statement about the general biquadratic quartic $B_{a,b}(x)=x^4-2(a+b)x^2+(a-b)^2$. Over any field of characteristic different from $2$ with $a\ne b$, it has a root if and only if both $a$ and $b$ are squares. Its roots are then exactly $\pm\alpha\pm\beta$, and these are four distinct elements. Over a finite field, $B_{a,b}$ therefore has $4$ or $0$ roots. Specialising to $f$ via quadratic reciprocity, we show that for every prime $p\ge5$, $f$ has $4$ roots modulo $p$ if $p\equiv\pm1\pmod{24}$ and none otherwise. No proper divisor of $24$ determines this type, and $f$ is irreducible over $\Q$ although it is a product of two monic quadratics modulo every prime. Viewing the type $T$ as a random variable and the residue $p\bmod24$ as a side channel, we prove full pinning, $I(T;p\bmod 24)=H(T)$, on every finite set of primes $p\ge5$. We compute the class-level entropy exactly, $H(T)=2-\frac34\lb3\in(0.8109,0.8114)$. For semiprimes $N=pq$ we show that $N\bmod24$ carries exactly $\frac{19}{8}-\frac{21}{16}\lb3\approx0.2947$ bits about the type pair. It carries exactly $0$ bits about which factor of a mixed semiprime splits. The last statement is an instance of a general swap-symmetry law: an involution that preserves the side channel and flips a Boolean read-out forces zero mutual information. These exact values explain the finite-sample measurements $0.8074$, $0.2909$ and $0.0001$ bits reported in computational experiments.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Let $f\in\Z[x]$ be a monic irreducible polynomial and $p$ a prime. The \emph{splitting type} of $f$ at $p$ describes how $f$ factors modulo $p$. By the Frobenius and Chebotarev density theorems, it is governed by the conjugacy class of a Frobenius element in the Galois group of the splitting field. When that group is abelian, class field theory says more. The Frobenius class depends only on $p$ modulo a fixed integer $m$, the \emph{conductor}. The type is then a deterministic function of $p\bmod m$.

This paper studies the smallest interesting non-cyclic abelian example, the biquadratic field $K=\Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt3)$, from two angles.

\emph{Arithmetic.} We prove from first principles, with explicit algebraic identities, that the splitting type of $f(x)=x^4-10x^2+1$ takes only two values and is determined by $p\bmod24$. We also prove that no smaller modulus works and that $f$ is irreducible over $\Q$ but reducible modulo every prime.

\emph{Information.} We treat a prime as a message, its type $T$ as a hidden variable, and a residue as an observable side channel. We then compute Shannon entropies and mutual informations exactly, in closed form involving only $\lb3$. The computations cover
(i) the entropy of the type;
(ii) the information that $p\bmod24$ carries about $T$ (all of it);
(iii) the information that $N=pq\bmod 24$ carries about the type pair of a semiprime; and
(iv) the information that $N\bmod24$ carries about \emph{which} factor of a mixed semiprime splits (none of it).
Item (iv) follows from a purely combinatorial \emph{swap-symmetry law}.

Computational experiments on finite samples reported $H(T)\approx0.8074$, a pair-channel value of $\approx0.2909$, and a which-factor value of $\approx0.0001$ bits. Our exact class-level values are $0.8113\ldots$, $0.2947\ldots$ and $0$. These are the limits under uniform sampling of residue classes, which by Dirichlet's theorem is the asymptotic distribution of primes.

\section{Definitions and notation}

Information is measured in bits.

\begin{definition}[Uniform entropy of a read-out]
Let $S$ be a nonempty finite set and $g:S\to V$. For $v\in g(S)$ put $n_v=\#\{x\in S:g(x)=v\}$. The \emph{entropy of $g$ on $S$} is
\[ H_S(g)=\sum_{v\in g(S)}\frac{n_v}{|S|}\lb\frac{|S|}{n_v}, \]
and $H_\emptyset(g)=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Conditional entropy, mutual information]
For a side channel $k:S\to W$ and $c\in k(S)$ let $S_c=k^{-1}(c)$. Define
\[ H_S(g\mid k)=\sum_{c\in k(S)}\frac{|S_c|}{|S|}H_{S_c}(g),\qquad I_S(g;k)=H_S(g)-H_S(g\mid k). \]
We say $k$ \emph{fully pins} $g$ on $S$ if $I_S(g;k)=H_S(g)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Factoring implies pinning]\label{lem:factor}
If $k(x)=k(y)$ implies $g(x)=g(y)$ for all $x,y\in S$, then $I_S(g;k)=H_S(g)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$g$ is constant on each nonempty fibre $S_c$, so $H_{S_c}(g)=0$ and $H_S(g\mid k)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Count formula]\label{lem:count}
If the fibres of $g$ have sizes $n_1,\dots,n_r>0$ with $\sum n_i=N=|S|$, then $H_S(g)=\lb N-\frac1N\sum_i n_i\lb n_i$.
\end{lemma}

Let $h(t)=-t\lb t-(1-t)\lb(1-t)$. Then $h(\tfrac14)=2-\tfrac34\lb3$.

Let $U_{24}=\{1,5,7,11,13,17,19,23\}$ represent $(\Z/24\Z)^\times$. Every prime $p\ge5$ has $p\bmod24\in U_{24}$, and every $r\in U_{24}$ satisfies $r^2\equiv1\pmod{24}$, so $(\Z/24)^\times\cong C_2^3$. Define $\tau:U_{24}\to\{0,4\}$ by $\tau(r)=4$ if $r\in\{1,23\}$ and $\tau(r)=0$ otherwise.

\section{The general biquadratic quartic over a field}

Let $F$ be a field, $a,b\in F$, and $B_{a,b}(x)=x^4-2(a+b)x^2+(a-b)^2$. Then $B_{2,3}(x)=x^4-10x^2+1$.

\begin{lemma}[Three difference-of-squares shapes]\label{lem:shapes}
For all $x\in F$,
\[ B_{a,b}(x)=(x^2+a-b)^2-4ax^2=(x^2-a+b)^2-4bx^2=(x^2-a-b)^2-4ab. \]
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Root criterion]\label{thm:rootcrit}
If $2\ne0$ in $F$ and $a\neq b$, then $B_{a,b}$ has a root in $F$ if and only if both $a$ and $b$ are squares in $F$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $B_{a,b}(x)=0$ and $x=0$, then $(a-b)^2=0$, which is impossible. So $2x\ne0$. The first shape gives $a=\big((x^2+a-b)/(2x)\big)^2$, and the second gives $b=\big((x^2-a+b)/(2x)\big)^2$. Conversely, if $a=\alpha^2$ and $b=\beta^2$, then $\alpha+\beta$ is a root by Proposition~\ref{prop:rootset}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Root set]\label{prop:rootset}
If $\alpha^2=a$ and $\beta^2=b$, then $B_{a,b}(x)=0$ if and only if $x\in\{\pm\alpha\pm\beta\}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
By the third shape, $B_{a,b}(x)=(x^2-(\alpha+\beta)^2)(x^2-(\alpha-\beta)^2)=(x-\alpha-\beta)(x+\alpha+\beta)(x-\alpha+\beta)(x+\alpha-\beta)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Distinctness]\label{prop:distinct}
If $2\ne0$, $\alpha,\beta\ne0$ and $\alpha^2\ne\beta^2$, the four elements $\pm\alpha\pm\beta$ are pairwise distinct.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Each pairwise difference is, up to sign, $2\alpha$, $2\beta$, $2(\alpha+\beta)$ or $2(\alpha-\beta)$, and none of these is zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two types over a finite field]\label{thm:twotypes-field}
Let $F$ be finite with $2\neq0$, and let $a,b\ne0$ with $a\neq b$. Then $B_{a,b}$ has exactly $4$ roots in $F$ if $a$ and $b$ are both squares, and $0$ roots otherwise.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Reducibility from a quadratic subfield]\label{prop:reducible}
If one of $a,b,ab$ is a square in $F$, then $B_{a,b}$ is a product of two monic quadratics:
\begin{align*}
a=\alpha^2 &: B_{a,b}=(x^2-2\alpha x+a-b)(x^2+2\alpha x+a-b),\\
b=\beta^2 &: B_{a,b}=(x^2-2\beta x-a+b)(x^2+2\beta x-a+b),\\
ab=\gamma^2 &: B_{a,b}=(x^2-a-b-2\gamma)(x^2-a-b+2\gamma).
\end{align*}
\end{proposition}

\section{The quartic $x^4-10x^2+1$ modulo primes}

\begin{lemma}[Second supplement]
For odd $p$, $2$ is a square modulo $p$ if and only if $p\equiv\pm1\pmod8$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Character of 3]\label{lem:three}
For a prime $p\ge5$, $3$ is a square modulo $p$ if and only if $p\equiv\pm1\pmod{12}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By quadratic reciprocity: if $p\equiv1\pmod4$, then $3$ is a square mod $p$ iff $p\equiv1\pmod3$. If $p\equiv3\pmod4$, it is a square iff $p\equiv2\pmod3$, since $2$ is not a square mod $3$. The Chinese remainder theorem combines these into $p\equiv1,11\pmod{12}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Root criterion at conductor 24]
For a prime $p\ge5$, $x^4-10x^2+1\equiv0\pmod p$ is solvable if and only if $p\equiv\pm1\pmod{24}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:rootcrit}, both $2$ and $3$ must be squares, so $p\equiv\pm1\pmod8$ and $p\equiv\pm1\pmod{12}$. Among the units modulo $24$ this means $p\equiv1,23$.
\end{proof}

Define $T(p)=\#\{x\in\F_p:x^4-10x^2+1=0\}$.

\begin{theorem}[Only two types]\label{thm:twotypes}
For every prime $p\ge5$, $T(p)=\tau(p\bmod24)$. That is, $T(p)=4$ if $p\equiv\pm1\pmod{24}$ and $T(p)=0$ otherwise.
\end{theorem}

For example, $T(23)=4$ with roots $2,11,12,21$; $T(47)=4$ with roots $5,19,28,42$; and $T(73)=4$ with roots $11,20,53,62$.

\begin{theorem}[The conductor is exactly 24]
For every divisor $d\neq24$ of $24$, there are primes $p,q\ge5$ with $p\equiv q\pmod d$ and $T(p)\ne T(q)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each proper divisor divides $8$ or $12$. Use $(7,23)$ (congruent mod $8$, types $0,4$) or $(13,73)$ (congruent mod $12$, types $0,4$).
\end{proof}

\section{Globally irreducible, locally reducible everywhere}

\begin{theorem}[Reducible modulo every prime]
For every prime $p$, $x^4-10x^2+1$ is a product of two monic quadratics in $\F_p[x]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:reducible}, it suffices that one of $2,3,6$ is a square. This is trivial for $p=2,3$. For $p\ge5$, if $\big(\frac2p\big)=\big(\frac3p\big)=-1$ then $\big(\frac6p\big)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Irreducible over $\Q$]
$x^4-10x^2+1$ is irreducible in $\Z[x]$, hence in $\Q[x]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A monic linear factor $x+c$ would give $c(10c-c^3)=1$, so $c=\pm1$, but $1-10+1\neq0$. A factorisation $(x^2+ux+v)(x^2+wx+z)$ forces $vz=1$. Evaluating at $\pm1,\pm2$ then gives $w=-u$ and $u^2\in\{12,8\}$, which is impossible over $\Z$. Gauss's lemma passes to $\Q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Local--global contrast]
$x^4-10x^2+1$ is irreducible over $\Q$ but reducible modulo every prime.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
For $p\nmid6$, the factorisation type is the cycle type of Frobenius on the roots. $V_4$ contains no $4$-cycle, so only the types $(1,1,1,1)$ and $(2,2)$ occur.
\end{remark}

\section{Type entropy and full pinning}

\begin{theorem}[Full pinning]
For every finite set $S$ of primes $\ge5$, $I_S(T;p\bmod24)=H_S(T)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:twotypes}, $T$ factors through $p\bmod24$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:factor}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact class-level entropy]\label{thm:entropy}
$H_{U_{24}}(\tau)=2-\frac34\lb3=h(\tfrac14)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The fibres have sizes $2$ and $6$, so $H=3-\frac18(2+6\lb6)=2-\frac34\lb3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Rational bounds on $\lb3$]\label{lem:logbounds}
$\frac{84}{53}<\lb3<\frac{485}{306}$, equivalently $2^{84}<3^{53}$ and $3^{306}<2^{485}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{corollary}[Numerical window]
$0.8109<H_{U_{24}}(\tau)<0.8114$. In particular, the finite-sample value $0.8074$ lies strictly below the limit.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Class-level pinning]
$I_{U_{24}}(\tau;\mathrm{id})=H_{U_{24}}(\tau)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
An empirical entropy below $h(\frac14)$ means the empirical split frequency is below $\frac14$, since $h$ increases on $[0,\frac12]$. This is consistent with Chebyshev's bias: among the units modulo $24$ only the class $1$ is a square.
\end{remark}

\section{The semiprime pair channel}

On $U_{24}^2$ (64 ordered pairs) let $\pi(r,s)=(\tau(r),\tau(s))$ and $\kappa(r,s)=rs\bmod24$.

\begin{proposition}[Pair entropy]
$H_{U_{24}^2}(\pi)=4-\frac32\lb3=2h(\frac14)$. The fibres have sizes $4,12,12,36$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{lemma}[Uniform product fibres]
For each $c\in U_{24}$, exactly $8$ pairs satisfy $rs\equiv c$, namely $\{(r,rc):r\in U_{24}\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Fibre entropies]\label{lem:fibres}
The entropy of $\pi$ on the fibre over $c$ is $2-\frac34\lb3$ if $c\in\{1,23\}$ and $\frac32$ otherwise.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $H_0=\{1,23\}$. If $c\in H_0$, then $r\in H_0\iff rc\in H_0$, giving the distribution $(2,6)$. If $c\notin H_0$, exactly one of $r,rc$ lies in $H_0$ for $r\in H_0\cup cH_0$ (two pairs each way), and neither does for the other four values. This gives the distribution $(2,2,4)$ with entropy $\frac32$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Semiprime pair channel]
$I_{U_{24}^2}(\pi;\kappa)=\frac{19}{8}-\frac{21}{16}\lb3\approx0.2947$ bits.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each fibre has weight $\frac18$, so
$H(\pi\mid\kappa)=\frac68\cdot\frac32+\frac28(2-\frac34\lb3)$. Therefore
$I=4-\frac32\lb3-\frac98-\frac12+\frac3{16}\lb3=\frac{19}8-\frac{21}{16}\lb3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$0.2943<I_{U_{24}^2}(\pi;\kappa)<0.2951$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The swap-symmetry law}

\begin{lemma}[Balanced bit]\label{lem:balanced}
Let $t$ be a nonempty finite set, $g:t\to\{0,1\}$, and $\sigma:t\to t$ an involution with $g(\sigma x)=1-g(x)$. Then the two fibres of $g$ are in bijection via $\sigma$, and $H_t(g)=1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Swap-symmetry law]\label{thm:swap}
Let $S$ be finite, $g:S\to\{0,1\}$, $k:S\to W$, and $\sigma:S\to S$ with $\sigma\circ\sigma=\mathrm{id}$, $g\circ\sigma=1-g$ and $k\circ\sigma=k$. Then $I_S(g;k)=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The map $\sigma$ preserves every fibre $S_c$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:balanced} gives $H_{S_c}(g)=1$ for every $c$. Hence $H_S(g\mid k)=1=H_S(g)$.
\end{proof}

Let $M\subset U_{24}^2$ be the $24$ mixed pairs, in which exactly one coordinate has $\tau=4$, and let $g(r,s)=1$ if and only if $\tau(r)=4$.

\begin{theorem}[Which-factor information vanishes]
$I_M(g;\kappa)=0$, while $H_M(g)=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:swap} with $\sigma(r,s)=(s,r)$. Mixedness is symmetric, swapping flips $g$, and $rs=sr$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The same argument applies to every finite sample of mixed prime pairs that is closed under swapping. If the sample is not swap-closed, for example if one keeps only pairs with $p<q$, a small nonzero value can appear. Numerically, for mixed pairs $5\le p<q\le3000$ the which-factor information is about $0.0003$ bits, while it is $0$ on all ordered mixed pairs from the same range. A reported value such as $0.0001$ bits is consistent with this.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Type of a prime]
Given $p\ge5$, return $4$ if $p\bmod24\in\{1,23\}$, else $0$. To certify a split prime, compute $\alpha^2\equiv2$ and $\beta^2\equiv3$ and list $\pm\alpha\pm\beta$. To certify a non-split prime, exhibit a square root of one of $2,3,6$ and the factorisation of Proposition~\ref{prop:reducible}.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Exact channel entropies]
Build the joint histogram of $(g,k)$ in $O(|S|)$ time and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:count} to $S$ and to every fibre.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Swap-symmetry certificate]
Check the three conditions of Theorem~\ref{thm:swap} pointwise in $O(|S|)$ time. If they hold, conclude $I=0$ exactly.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Discussion}

The measured values $0.8074$, $0.2909$ and $0.0001$ bits correspond to the exact class-level values $h(\frac14)=0.81128\ldots$, $\frac{19}{8}-\frac{21}{16}\lb3=0.29474\ldots$ and $0$. The zero holds exactly on every finite sample that is closed under swapping the factors. In factoring-based cryptography, any function of $N=pq$ alone is invariant under $p\leftrightarrow q$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:swap}, no such function reveals any order-sensitive Boolean property that the swap flips. The two-type phenomenon reflects the structure of $V_4$, in which the identity and the three involutions occur in proportion $1:3$. Only the characters of $2$ and $3$ carry number-theoretic content; the rest of the algebra is valid over any field of characteristic $\ne2$.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Conductor law for $\Q(\sqrt a,\sqrt b)$.} For squarefree coprime $a,b$, the type of $B_{a,b}$ at $p\nmid2ab(a-b)$ should depend exactly on $p\bmod\operatorname{lcm}(f_a,f_b)$, where $f_d$ is the conductor of $\Q(\sqrt d)$.
\item \emph{Universality of the $V_4$ entropy.} Every biquadratic field should have class-level type entropy $h(\frac14)$.
\item \emph{Swap-blindness for all abelian channels.} For every abelian conductor $m$, the residue $pq\bmod m$ should carry zero which-factor information.
\item \emph{Multiquadratic pair channel.} For the group $C_2^k$ with $n=2^k$, the predicted value is
\[ I_n=\Big(2-\frac1n\Big)h\Big(\frac1n\Big)-\frac{n-1}{n}H\Big(\frac1n,\frac1n,1-\frac2n\Big), \]
which recovers $\frac{19}{8}-\frac{21}{16}\lb3$ for $n=4$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

A threefold difference-of-squares identity reduces $x^4-10x^2+1$ to two quadratic characters, and reciprocity places these on clocks of $8$ and $12$ hours whose least common multiple, $24$, is exactly the conductor. The residue modulo $24$ pins the type completely. For semiprimes, the product residue reveals $\frac{19}{8}-\frac{21}{16}\lb3$ bits about the type pair and, by commutativity, nothing about which factor splits.

